<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-damping-summary" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Damping cases and the constants c, m, k</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ASSEMBLAGE: SUMMARY TABLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-damping-summary">
    <title>Summary of Behavior: <m>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0</m></title>
    <p>
      We solve this ODE by solving the roots of the characteristic equation:
      <me>mr^2 + cr + k = 0.</me>
      The behavior of solutions is determined entirely by the discriminant
      <m>D = c^2 - 4km</m>, which controls the type of roots of the characteristic
      equation.
    </p>
    <tabular halign="left" row-headers="yes">
      <col width="22%"/>
      <col width="36%"/>
      <col width="20%"/>
      <col width="22%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>Case</cell>
        <cell>Discriminant and roots</cell>
        <cell>Crossings of <m>x=0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Overdamped</term> (larger <m>c</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>c^2 > 4km</m> (two distinct real roots)</cell>
        <cell>At most once</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Critically damped</term> (borderline)</cell>
        <cell><m>c^2 = 4km</m> (one repeated real root)</cell>
        <cell>At most once</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Underdamped</term> (smaller <m>c</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>0 \lt c^2 \lt 4km</m> (complex roots with nonzero real part)</cell>
        <cell>Infinitely many</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Undamped</term> (<m>c = 0</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>c = 0</m> (pure imaginary roots)</cell>
        <cell>Infinitely many</cell>
        <cell>DNE</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>
      In all <em>damped</em> cases (<m>c > 0</m>), the mass eventually returns
      to equilibrium: <m>\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t) = 0</m>. In the undamped case
      (<m>c = 0</m>) the mass oscillates forever with constant amplitude.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC 1: IDENTIFY CASE FROM NUMERICAL m, c, k
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-identify-case">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A spring-mass-dashpot system has <m>m = 1</m>, <m>c = 3</m>, <m>k = 4</m>.
        Which damping case is this?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Underdamped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The discriminant is <m>c^2 - 4km = 9 - 16 = -7 \lt 0</m>,
            so the characteristic equation has complex roots and the system is
            underdamped. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Overdamped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Overdamped requires <m>c^2 > 4km</m>. Here <m>c^2 - 4km = 9 - 16 = -7 \lt 0</m>,
            so this is not overdamped.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Critically damped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Critically damped requires <m>c^2 = 4km</m>. Here <m>c^2 - 4km = -7 \neq 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Undamped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Undamped means <m>c = 0</m>. Here <m>c = 3 \neq 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC 2: WHICH CASES GIVE INFINITELY MANY CROSSINGS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-infinitely-many-crossings">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The mass passes through the equilibrium position infinitely many times.
        Select <em>all</em> cases this could correspond to.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Underdamped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Underdamped solutions oscillate with decaying amplitude and
            cross <m>x = 0</m> infinitely many times. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Undamped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The undamped system oscillates with constant amplitude and
            also crosses <m>x = 0</m> infinitely many times. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Overdamped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Overdamped solutions pass through equilibrium at most once. They cannot
            cross infinitely many times.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Critically damped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Critically damped solutions also pass through equilibrium at most once.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC 3: COMPLEX ROOTS MEANS UNDAMPED OR UNDERDAMPED
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: TWO PARTS (PAGE 28)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-damping-identify-c">
    <title>Identifying the Damping Case</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that the position of a mass in a spring-mass-dashpot system is
        modeled by the ODE:
        <me>x'' + cx' + 4x = 0, \quad x(0) = 1,\quad x'(0) = 0.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            For what values of <m>c</m> will the mass oscillate and pass through
            the equilibrium position infinitely many times?
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Suppose <m>c = 4</m>. Can you make the mass oscillate and pass through
            the equilibrium position infinitely many times by choosing a different
            value for <m>x'(0)</m>?
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For part (a): which damping cases cause the mass to pass through equilibrium
        infinitely many times? What conditions on <m>c</m> correspond to those cases?
      </p>
      <p>
        For part (b): the damping case depends only on an inequality involving
        <m>c</m>, <m>m</m>, and <m>k</m>. If these are fixed, does changing the
        initial conditions affect which case we are in?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Part (a).</em> From the summary above, the mass passes through
        equilibrium infinitely many times when the system is underdamped or undamped.
        Here <m>m = 1</m> and <m>k = 4</m>, so the discriminant is
        <m>D = c^2 - 4km = c^2 - 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Underdamped requires <m>0 \lt c^2 \lt 4km</m>, i.e. <m>0 \lt c^2 \lt 16</m>,
        which gives <m>0 \lt c \lt 4</m>.
        Undamped requires <m>c = 0</m>.
        Combining, the mass oscillates infinitely many times when
        <me>0 \leq c \lt 4.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Part (b).</em> No. The damping case is determined entirely by
        <m>c^2 - 4km</m>, which depends only on <m>m</m>, <m>c</m>, and <m>k</m>.
        With <m>c = 4</m>, <m>m = 1</m>, <m>k = 4</m> we have
        <m>D = 16 - 16 = 0</m>, so the system is critically damped regardless
        of the initial conditions. A critically damped system passes through
        equilibrium at most once, so no choice of <m>x'(0)</m> can produce
        infinitely many crossings.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<exercise xml:id="ex-mc-complex-roots-cases">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The characteristic equation of a spring-mass system has complex roots.
        Which case or cases could this correspond to?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Undamped or underdamped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Complex roots arise in two situations. When <m>c = 0</m> the
            roots are purely imaginary (zero real part) and the system is undamped.
            When <m>c > 0</m> and <m>c^2 \lt 4km</m> the roots have a nonzero
            negative real part and the system is underdamped. In both cases the
            roots are complex. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Underdamped only</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Underdamped is one correct case, but not the only one. When <m>c = 0</m>
            the characteristic equation also has complex (purely imaginary) roots,
            giving the undamped case.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Overdamped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Overdamped corresponds to two distinct <em>real</em> roots, not
            complex roots.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Critically damped</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Critically damped corresponds to one repeated <em>real</em> root,
            not complex roots.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
 <conclusion>
  <title>Key Takeaways</title>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>
        <alert>The damping case tells us:</alert> how many times the mass crosses
        the equilibrium position and the long-term behavior <m>\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t)</m>.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>The damping case is determined by the type of roots of the characteristic equation,</alert>
        which can be found by computing the discriminant <m>D = c^2 - 4km</m>.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Initial conditions do not change the case:</alert> <m>m</m>, <m>c</m>,
        and <m>k</m> alone determine whether the system is overdamped, critically damped, or underdamped.
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Complex roots arise in two cases:</alert> purely imaginary roots
        (<m>c = 0</m>, undamped) and complex roots with negative real part
        (<m>c > 0</m> and <m>c^2 \lt 4km</m>, underdamped).
      </p></li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

